# Audio Policy

## Purpose

Enable safe, catalog-driven audio enhancement during creative remix work.

Audio is conditionally allowed when it is sourced from the trusted audio catalog and integrated without breaking gameplay, controls, boot flow, or browser autoplay behavior.

## Trusted Audio Catalog

Workers must read the task packet field:

```text
audio_catalog_path
```

This path points to a copied `audios.json` file containing grouped CDN-backed audio variants.

The canonical source catalog is:

```text
assets/audio_library/audios.json
```

The only approved remote audio prefix is:

```text
https://gamezone-virid.vercel.app/assets/audios/
```

## Catalog Rules

- Use only URLs listed in `audio_catalog_path`.
- Do not invent, fetch, search for, or synthesize new remote audio URLs.
- Do not use arbitrary remote audio hosts.
- Do not use local absolute filesystem paths in final game code.
- If a desired event has no exact match, choose the closest semantic group from the catalog or skip that sound.
- Missing or failed audio playback must never break gameplay.

## Integration Goals

Add as many semantically appropriate sounds as practical while preserving mechanics.

Preferred mappings:

```text
starts / clicks / hovers / menu_opens / menu_closes -> UI interactions
jumps / lands -> movement
shoots / projectiles -> firing, casting, spawned projectiles
hits -> generic collisions
damages -> player hurt feedback
enemy_hits / enemy_defeats -> enemy combat feedback
pickups / bonuses / powerups -> rewards and upgrades
blocked_actions / warnings / failures -> invalid, danger, or failed actions
successes / level_completes / game_overs -> state transitions
ambient_loops / action_loops -> gameplay background loops
victory_loops / defeat_loops -> result screens
soft_magic_loops / spooky_loops / arcade_loops -> atmosphere-specific ambience
```

## Runtime Safety Rules

- Initialize audio only after a user gesture when required by browser autoplay policy.
- Keep loop volume modest and one-shot volume balanced.
- Reuse a small helper function instead of scattering raw playback logic everywhere.
- Use `audio.play().catch(() => {})` or equivalent safe handling.
- Do not block startup, rendering, input, or win/loss logic on audio loading.
- Do not add audio that obscures or changes mechanics.
- Do not remove existing gameplay behavior to add sound.

## Recommended Implementation Pattern

```js
const REMIX_AUDIO = {
  pickup: "https://gamezone-virid.vercel.app/assets/audios/sfx/pickup_01.mp3",
  damage: "https://gamezone-virid.vercel.app/assets/audios/sfx/damage_01.mp3"
};

function playRemixSound(key, volume = 0.45) {
  const url = REMIX_AUDIO[key];
  if (!url) return;
  try {
    const audio = new Audio(url);
    audio.volume = volume;
    audio.play().catch(() => {});
  } catch (_) {}
}
```

For loops:

```js
let remixLoopAudio;

function startRemixLoop(url, volume = 0.25) {
  if (remixLoopAudio || !url) return;
  try {
    remixLoopAudio = new Audio(url);
    remixLoopAudio.loop = true;
    remixLoopAudio.volume = volume;
    remixLoopAudio.play().catch(() => {});
  } catch (_) {}
}
```

## Evidence Expectations

When audio is added, summarize:

```text
- which game events received audio
- which catalog groups were used
- whether loop playback waits for user gesture
- whether all audio URLs came from the trusted catalog
```
